The PRKN gene encodes parkin, an E3 ubiquitin ligase involved in mitophagy and mitochondrial quality control. Mutations in PRKN are associated with PARK2 gene mutations, which cause parkinsonism. The MFN1 and MFN2 genes encode mitofusins, proteins involved in mitochondrial fusion. Defects in MFN1 and MFN2 are linked to mitochondrial dysfunction and autosomal recessive spastic ataxia with peripheral neuropathy (ARSPA). 

PRKN mutations contribute to Parkinson's disease (PD), a neurodegenerative disorder characterized by dopaminergic neuron loss and motor deficits. Parkin plays a critical role in mitochondrial quality control and proteasomal degradation. Dysfunction in PRKN can lead to impaired mitophagy, accumulation of damaged mitochondria, and oxidative stress, which are hallmark features of PD. Therefore, the PRKN allele contributes to Parkinson's disease. 

